This is a frustrating but well-known phenomenon called the "Optimizer Paradox."

  When statistics are vague, the optimizer is cautious and often defaults to a "safe" Nested Loop. When you run ANALYZE, the optimizer becomes "confident" in its math. If
  it sees a high volume of data (or incorrectly calculates a high volume due to a lack of multi-column statistics), it "proves" to itself that a Hash Join is
  mathematically superior, even though in reality, the Nested Loop is faster for your specific row.

  Since you cannot "copy-paste" a plan in PostgreSQL, here are the three ways to force Customer 544 to use the "320-style" plan.

  Option 1: pg_hint_plan (The Surgical Approach)

  This is the industry standard for forcing plans in Postgres. It allows you to add a comment to your SQL that the database treats as a command.

  How to do it:
  You add a hint at the top of your 3,000-line query. To force the Nested Loop behavior seen in the 320 plan:

  /*+
      NestedLoop(cc ccd)
      NestedLoop(cc cc_4)
      NestedLoop(cc frc)
      IndexScan(cc)
   */
  SELECT ... -- your 3000 line query
  - Pros: Only affects this specific query; very precise.
  - Cons: Requires the pg_hint_plan extension to be installed on the DB server.

  ---

  Option 2: Session-Level "Brute Force" (The Quick Fix)

  If you cannot install extensions and need a fix right now, you can tell PostgreSQL to "forget" that Hash Joins and Merge Joins exist for the duration of that specific
  session. This forces the optimizer to fall back to Nested Loops.

  Run these commands immediately before executing the query:
  SET enable_hashjoin = off;
  SET enable_mergejoin = off;

  -- Now run your query
  SELECT ... FROM ... WHERE customer_id = 544;

  -- Reset them back to default afterwards
  RESET enable_hashjoin;
  RESET enable_mergejoin;
  - Pros: No installation required; guaranteed to stop the Hash/Merge joins.
  - Cons: If the 3,000-line query has some parts that actually need a Hash Join to be fast, those parts will also slow down.

  ---

  Option 3: plan_cache_mode (The Application Fix)

  If your application uses Prepared Statements (which most Java/Python/Node apps do), Postgres might be using a "Generic Plan."

  Try setting this at the database or user level:
  ALTER DATABASE your_db_name SET plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan;
  This forces Postgres to re-evaluate the plan for the specific value (544) every single time, rather than trying to use a "one size fits all" plan that is optimized for
  "average" customers.

